{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"DECISION REVISITED + FINALIZED (2026-08-18, supersedes the deep-dive's \"check-not-generate + scoped test\" recommendation). Two operator rulings collapsed the remaining ambiguity:\n\nRULING 1 — force through ANY surface, audited by the ABSENCE of a signed attestation. A force op produces no signed attestation (gate bypassed / closed-without-signature); that missing signature IS the durable audit signal, and projects that want to prevent force from circumventing protections check for certification in their CI pipeline. Consequence: MCP must GAIN force parity; the prior \"force is CLI-only\" governance is RESCINDED. This removes the last \"intended divergence\".\nRULING 2 — to keep parity exception-free, REMOVE deprecated flags/verbs that would fail the oracle rather than exempt them. So force_close (deprecated) is NOT mirrored to MCP and NOT given an oracle exception — it is removed from the input surface. Same principle for any other deprecated write verb/flag.\n\nNET: the write-op param surface has ZERO intended divergences. The oracle asserts TOTAL parity across lib / CLI / MCP with NO capability flags and NO allow-list. This is the cleanest form of the OSS conformance-oracle pattern and fully retires the earlier Frankenstein risk (no generation+conformance+allow-list blend).\n\nMECHANISM (Pattern B — behavioral conformance oracle, a single proven OSS pattern):\n- ONE transport-agnostic behavioral contract = a table of (op, inputs, expected accept/reject + expected effect). Run it through each adapter (lib, CLI, MCP) against a temp store. Precedent: DB-API / SQLAlchemy dialect compliance, CommonMark spec suite, WSGI/ASGI validators, S3-compat & k8s CRI/CSI conformance suites.\n- It tests BEHAVIOR/CONTRACT (accept/reject/effect), so it catches the real risk (G4 runtime conditional-required rules: close_class required iff bug-close; reason conditionally required per REASON_REQUIRED_CLASSES) that shape-generation provably cannot. It is NOT a change-detector and NOT an NxM matrix: one authored contract, N adapters; new adapter reruns the same suite; new op adds one spec row; drift in ANY adapter fails automatically.\n- Divergence == a FAILING conformance case (a bug to converge), not an allow-listed exception.\n\nWHY NOT Pattern A (generate the surfaces): behavior is ALREADY single-sourced (all surfaces funnel into composer.create_core / transition_compute / claim_compute), so codegen buys little; the surfaces carry irreducible hand-written value (LLM-facing MCP docstrings; CLI ergonomics); generation CANNOT express the runtime conditional-required rules (G4); and regenerating CLI/MCP would risk user/agent-visible contract breaks (flag spellings, exit code 10, LLM tool schema). Pattern A is retired for the write path.\n\nREMEDIATION (proposed split-out tickets, discovered_from cranial-goodly-seahog):\n1. MCP write-param parity BUG — transition_ticket/claim_ticket gain force + reason + caused_by + ref (NOT force_close). Update the MCP docstrings that assert \"intentionally NO force bypass\".\n2. Behavioral conformance ORACLE — the accept/reject+effect suite over lib/CLI/MCP adapters, temp-store driven, zero exceptions. This is the durable parity gate.\n3. Remove deprecated write-path surface — force_close (and any deprecated verb/flag) via the supported rebar._deprecations tombstone/warn path. CAVEAT (event-sourcing): force_close lives in the EVENT SCHEMA + reducer (ticket_state.schema.json, _processors_status.py, txn.py) — remove the INPUT surface (lib param, CLI close --force) but the reducer must keep tolerating historical force_close events (tombstone, NOT hard delete).\n4. CLI standardization (SEPARATE, orthogonal) — the CLI is a hand-rolled arg loop (bespoke --force default-reason, --assignee None-sentinel-vs-clear). Move toward a standard framework (argparse/Click/Typer) for consistency + accurate --help, as maintained OSS CLIs do. This is an ADAPTER refactor; the oracle then simply validates the new CLI too. It does NOT blend with the oracle (avoids Frankenstein).\n5. Docs — rewrite AGENTS.md's \"--force is CLI-only / not exposed over MCP\" governance to the new model (force everywhere; audit = absent attestation; projects gate on certification in CI)."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787080290091182001,"uuid":"3975164d-1f62-432d-831e-c28b1baffc3e"}